class: MISCONFIG
asset: core.hypofriend.de/q, hypofriend.de/q (via https://hypofriend.de/crm inline client)
confidence: 74
reasoning: (1) The public static page https://hypofriend.de/crm (200, 7465B, S3/CloudFront) ships inline JS that does POST /q with body {"query":"query { root { form_authenticity_token } }"} and credentials:"include", then writes the value into a form input. That is the vendor's own code path for obtaining the CSRF token, and it proves `form_authenticity_token` is a live server-side field on the `root` type of the /q schema (absent from every prior mined op-map). (2) Re-verified live 2026-09-26 09:56Z on origin OPTIONS /en/plus/q with Origin: https://evil.example: access-control-allow-origin: https://evil.example, access-control-allow-credentials: true, all 7 methods, max-age 7200 — and the echo is a global rack-cors middleware, not path-scoped (arbitrary /zzz paths behave identically per prior cycles). (3) Session cookies are samesite=none; secure; httponly (re-confirmed this cycle on /api/v3/delete-admin-cookie: __hfp__, __hfp___hypofriend.health__, __hfp___hypofriend.protect__, __hfp___hypofriend.holistic__, __hfp___investfriend__, __hfp___hypofriend.plus__ all domain=hypofriend.de; samesite=none; httponly) — i.e. the app is deliberately configured to attach credentials on cross-site requests. (4) /crm is the "Login to Backoffice" staff CRM and consumes exactly this token in a POST form to /auth/google_oauth2. (5) Not a REJECTED class: this is a cross-origin *read* of a session-bound security token (confidentiality break), not CSRF on an anonymous or logout form.
evidence_needed: One authorized test, using a session the tester owns, showing a credentialed cross-origin POST carrying Origin: https://evil.example returns a formAuthenticityToken that differs from the same request without an Origin header or without cookies — i.e. that the value is session-bound rather than a constant. The program excludes exposing authentication data, so this must not be run by me.
verify_steps: Passive, re-runnable: `curl -sS https://hypofriend.de/crm | grep -A3 'fetch("/q"'` (expect the form_authenticity_token query with credentials:include); `curl -sSI -X OPTIONS https://core.hypofriend.de/en/plus/q -H 'Origin: https://evil.example' -H 'ACRM: POST' | grep -i 'access-control'` (expect ACAO echo + ACAC:true). The session-binding test itself is HUMAN-authorized only.
impact: An attacker-controlled site can read a victim's Rails CSRF token for hypofriend.de using only the victim's ambient cookies, then replay it in forged cross-origin requests. For a regulated mortgage app whose staff backoffice and lead/session cookies are all SameSite=None by design, this converts an app-wide CORS misconfiguration into a session-forgery primitive. HIGH if session-binding is confirmed; MEDIUM as evidenced (the read is proven, the forgery is not).
