## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

[HYP] Unauthenticated first-party referral links are minted with attacker-chosen campaign attribution that is then persisted client-side into lead state, so lead attribution has no server-side integrity binding
class: BUSLOGIC
asset: core.hypofriend.de (slug routes: /tools, /staff, /rails, /uploads, /karriere) → hypofriend.de/en/exchange
confidence: 60
reasoning: (1) Recognized slugs 301 to `hypofriend.de/en/exchange?share_id=…&link_id=…&redirect=…&utm_*` with a per-slug **fixed** `share_id`/`link_id` pair, verified by repeating `/tools` twice → identical UUIDs. The negative control set (`/admin`, `/internal`, `/sidekiq`, `/graphql`, `/backoffice`, 16 others) all falls to the canonical shell, so this is a real routed surface, not noise. (2) `utm_source`, `utm_medium`, `utm_campaign` and `redirect` are reflected **verbatim** from the request into the first-party HTTPS share URL, overriding the route's own defaults (`utm_source=email&utm_medium=free&utm_campaign=real_estate_v35`); `link_id` is unchanged across variants, so the server binds the link identity but not the campaign that identifies it. (3) `link_id` is a real persisted identifier, not a synthetic token: it resolves to a live auth-free `/en/account/property-search/expose-<link_id>` (200, 3766B). (4) The victim's own client closes the loop — bundle `XP()` reads `query.utm_*` off the landing URL and calls the `setMarketingCampaign` GraphQL mutation, then writes the values to sticky first-party localStorage keys `hypo_utm_source`/`hypo_utm_medium`/`hypo_utm_campaing` and session keys `hypofriend_user_web_session_utm_*`. (5) Counter-argument I am holding explicitly: UTM values are *meant* to be chosen by a campaign creator, so "attacker sets utm" is not itself a flaw. The finding is the **absence of any binding** — an already-issued official link's attribution can be silently re-pointed by appending one query parameter, and the poisoned value is made sticky on the victim's device. (6) The forgeable issuer is the direct Rails origin, which also carries no CloudFront security-header layer, so nothing upstream pins the values.
evidence_needed: Whether `utm_campaign`/`utm_medium` gate advisor routing, lead distribution or commission on the server. If they are stored on the lead record and consumed by the advisor/commission logic, forged-campaign links let an attacker capture or misroute lead attribution. Not obtainable passively — it needs one self-owned lead taken through a forged-campaign link and a read of that lead's stored attribution, and I will not write to live lead state.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -m 8 -o /dev/null -D - 'https://core.hypofriend.de/tools?utm_source=attacker&utm_medium=x&utm_campaign=evil&redirect=evil' | tr -d '\r' | grep -i '^location'` (expect utm_* echoed as `attacker`/`x`/`evil` with `link_id=cb8917e2-41d9-5ae9-bf82-d3a5ecc97705` unchanged); `curl -sS -m 8 -o /dev/null -D - 'https://core.hypofriend.de/tools' | tr -d '\r' | grep -i '^location'` (expect defaults `email`/`free`/`real_estate_v35`, same link_id — proves the pair is unbound); `curl -sS -m 8 -o /dev/null -D - 'https://core.hypofriend.de/admin' | tr -d '\r' | grep -i '^location'` (expect `https://hypofriend.de/`, the control that separates the two classes).
impact: An unauthenticated attacker can mint unlimited shareable first-party-looking Hypofriend referral URLs carrying arbitrary campaign/medium/source strings, and can retroactively overwrite the attribution on links Hypofriend itself issued, with the value made sticky in the victim's own browser and pushed to the server by the vendor's client. In a mortgage brokerage where lead attribution drives advisor assignment and commission, that is lead-routing and commission tampering on a money flow. MEDIUM as evidenced: the forgery and the client-side persistence are fully proven and reproducible; the server-side consequence (whether campaign gates payout) is the one unproven link. Not a data breach.
testability: PASSIVE

