## STEP 6 — LEARNING

- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ hypofriend.de/en/account/assets/index-SIiSbA_4.js**: a **separate SPA deploy** at `/en/account/assets/` (200, 2,468,740B) carries 43 named GraphQL operations / 35 mutations — `selectOffer`, `requestOfferRenewal`, `setOfferViewedByCustomerAt`, `sendVerifiedListingEmail`, `createUploadUrl`, `submitDocumentAnalysis`, `deleteLeadDocumentAttachment`, `createProperty`, `updateProperty`, `setPartnerForNewMortgage`, `setHomeBuyingStatusForLead`, `setDecisionAppointment`, 8 `set*ForAffordability` setters — **none present in the main `/m/_nuxt/` entry chunk**. The 4-cycle "bundle mining exhausted" conclusion is **withdrawn as a global claim**: it was measured over one asset path. The offer/lead write surface the standing findings only ever inferred is now enumerable from public static bytes.
- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED BUSLOGIC @ hypofriend.de/en/exchange**: `share_id` is a **DB record-existence gate, not a format check** — a well-formed non-existent UUID returns 404/511B, the two known share_ids resolve, and both known tokens are **published in plaintext** in the 301 `Location` of public stems. The gate therefore costs an attacker nothing, and cross-binding already proved share_id authorises nothing about the link. Confidence 88→93; this converts "authorization by obscurity" from inference to mechanism.
- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ core.hypofriend.de/en/exchange**: the forge executes on the **direct Rails origin** — 302 with the forged `link_id` and all three utm values echoed into `Location`, `x-frame-options: ALLOWALL`, no CloudFront in the path. The referral-forgery primitive is an origin-surface issue, not edge-only, consistent with the standing origin-bypass class. Both hosts set `cache-control: no-store`, so the cache facet is closed — but note the edge's 302 carries `vary: Accept-Encoding` only, so the 2026-09-25 `Vary: Origin` hardening **does not cover this route** and no-store is doing the work by accident.
- **[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ hypofriend.de/en/exchange (self-correction)**: my own prior "trusted-domain credential-phishing URL" claim is **withdrawn**. The `/en/account` destination is a 3766B `server: AmazonS3` shell, `XFO: DENY`, with no `__NUXT__` and no reflected campaign value, loading its own bundle rather than the main app. No attacker-controlled content exists at the destination; the finding is unbound attribution and unbounded link identity, not phishing. Recording the withdrawal explicitly is what keeps the remaining 93 honest.
- **[LEARN] REJECTED SSRF @ hypofriend.de/en/account**: `ChatLinkPreviewPost` is **not** a server-side fetch — `query ChatLinkPreviewPost($slug:String!,$locale:String!){ post(slug:$slug,locale:$locale){ title preview seo_description } }` is a CMS content lookup keyed by slug. Checked and cleared before it could harden into a false SSRF lead.
- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED NG @ api.hypofriend.de (target)**: `curl -I` connect-timeout at 12.0s on :443; `getent` clean at A 52.15.184.3 — dead **50th** consecutive cycle, no takeover surface, target unchanged. All activity this cycle was read-only GET/HEAD at ≤1rps plus one public static-asset fetch, and returned no customer data.

## STEP 7 — RISK

**[RISK] hypofriend: 92 — held, and this cycle is a good argument for why holding is right even though two findings improved.**

What rose: the referral forge's gate is now provably free, because the token is a DB-existence check and the vendor publishes valid tokens in plaintext on unauthenticated 301s — an attacker does not even need to steal one. And the forge is confirmed on the direct origin, so it sits outside the CloudFront WAF entirely.

What fell, and it fell because I checked it: the same finding's phishing sub-claim is dead. I asserted last cycle that the `/en/account` target produced a trusted-domain credential-phishing URL. The destination is a 3766B static S3 shell with no attacker-controlled content. Correcting my own prior overstatement is worth more than the point I would have gained by leaving it standing, because the 93 confidence on the surviving referral-forgery core is only credible if the inflated part is visibly removed.

The genuinely new material is the 35-mutation offer/lead write surface. That is the largest single unexamined region found in 27 cycles, it covers the offer and commission chain, and it was hidden behind the one asset path I had declared exhausted. It does not move a score whose ceiling is set by the untouched CRITICAL full-DB auth-free PII BOLA, and I will not credit discovery against customer data as severity — that number needs one authorized execution, which is a human gate.

The pattern across three cycles now: bundle mining on `/m/_nuxt/` returned nothing while I called it exhausted; path breadth returned the `/crm` discovery and the referral mint class; and parameter depth on a known endpoint returned this. The lesson generalises — the "exhausted" verdict was a statement about one path, not about the surface. The program should expect the same failure wherever I have generalised an exhaustion.

The POC phase remains pinned to `api.hypofriend.de`, now 50 consecutive cycles at 000, while every live finding sits on `hypofriend.de`. That mismatch has now cost enough measurable progress that re-pointing the phase remains the single highest-value action available — higher than any individual probe I can run.
