## STEP 5 — NEXT ACTION

- **[NEXT] PROBE**: `curl -sS -m 12 -c /tmp/j.txt -X POST https://core.hypofriend.de/q -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"query":"query { root { form_authenticity_token } }"}'` then re-issue the identical query twice **with `-b /tmp/j.txt`** and diff. Discriminator: if the token is **stable within a session but rotates per request**, it is a per-request `per_form_csrf_tokens` value and a cross-origin read is a per-request cost the attacker must pay in a loop; if it is **stable within a session**, the attacker pays one read and reuses it for the whole session, which is the difference between a nuisance and a reliable primitive. This is read-only against my own anonymous session, selects no customer field, and settles the exploit-cost question that H1's 78 currently leaves open.

## STEP 6 — LEARNING

- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED AUTH @ core.hypofriend.de/q + hypofriend.de/q + hypofriend.de/crm**: the credentialed-CORS finding is load-bearing in a way the base under-stated. The prior version said "session-token read primitive" on the strength of the CORS echo plus a token *inferred* from `/crm`'s inline JS. This cycle the server itself issues the token to an unauthenticated requester, returns it in the response body, and the vendor's own internal backoffice form fetches it from that endpoint. What changed is not the header but the payload: there is now a concrete, session-scoped secret sitting in a body that `ACAO` + `ACAC:true` + `samesite=none` makes readable cross-origin. I bounded it deliberately on three axes — the token is per-session (3 distinct hashes), the disclosure leg is proven while the replay leg is not, and `/auth/google_oauth2` stays dead so no backoffice bypass is claimed.
- **[LEARN] REJECTED IDOR @ core.hypofriend.de/q (`Lead.documents` cross-tenant read)**: I spent two cycles asserting an object-id write surface had a corresponding read path, and never tested the one thing that settles it. Today I mapped `documents` → `Document{id,type,file_name}` — a real document-id read path — and then watched it reject `lead_id` with `argumentNotAccepted`. The read path is session-bound, exactly like `root.lead`, which again today rejected `id`. So the cross-tenant chain is closed by my own control, and the hypothesis that carried 76 last cycle is revised to 55. Two different mistakes to record here, because they are the same mistake: last cycle I shipped `document_analysis_status` at 58 on an inference about argument shape without running the one cheap probe, and closed it only when I did run it. This cycle I let a "read path exists" inference stand across two cycles when the probe cost one request. The discipline that actually works is not reasoning harder about the bundle — it is spending the single request that returns a verdict.
- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED NG @ hypofriend.de/crm, core.hypofriend.de/en/plus/q**: the three `/q` instances are one schema, measured not assumed. `HypofriendType` with the identical 7 root fields and the identical 12-field `Lead` on both `/q` and `/en/plus/q`, against a three-week-old inventory note calling them distinct and unmapped. The consequence is that every hypothesis against one instance is now automatically a hypothesis against three, which retires the duplication rather than adding a finding. Separately, `root` has been losing fields — `calculateMaklerFee` gone since 2026-09-10, and now `calculateCityTax` and `jiyuCalculate` — so the main `/q` surface is shrinking while the appointment instances hold steady.
- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED NG @ hypofriend.de/m/_nuxt/-ez_sPea.js, hypofriend.de/en/account/assets/index-SIiSbA_4.js**: entry bundle renamed an 8th time in 10 days; account bundle bit-stable for the 5th cycle (2468740B, etag `3a85f704`), so it was not re-mined. Fifth consecutive cycle confirming the `/q` error channel, not the bundles, is the discovery source that still pays — this cycle's entire new surface came from asking the server what it would admit to.

## STEP 7 — RISK

- **[RISK]** hypofriend: **92 — held.** Nothing new exposed customer data: the one probe that could have touched a customer record returned `null` because the id matched nothing, and the `/en/account` bundle was not re-read at all. The number does not rise, and I want to be explicit that this is the fourth cycle running where the composition is moving but the magnitude is not. What changed is that a standing read primitive acquired a payload. Until this morning, "credentialed CORS echo" was a header finding with a plausible chain; the chain needed a session-scoped secret sitting in a cross-origin-readable body, and the server turns out to hand that out unauthenticated on request. That converts a configuration defect into an exploitable primitive against a platform holding applicant income, affordability and mortgage-document state, and it converts on a config line the vendor can fix without touching code. The three gates that remain — bulk PII quantification, ownership of a document id, attribution persistence on a lead — are all HUMAN_ONLY because each one requires writing to or reading from a live customer's record. I am not going to open one to make a number move, and I would rather report a 92 that a human can convert in an afternoon than a 95 I obtained by touching someone's mortgage application.
