---

**[HYP]**

**H1 — 5.5 · business-logic + unauth-paid-resource** (`index-BLX7yr-v.js`)
Because the entire system prompt and policy rules ship in public JS, any visitor's browser can drive the vendor's LLM account with attacker-chosen policy, and the model will state the wrong product-availability answers to a real lead. *Evidence:* agent IDs, `withCredentials:true`, identity forwarding, client-side prompt/CTA config, all in a file served pre-auth. *Refuted if:* `/api/v3/chatbase` rejects unauthenticated sessions server-side. *Auth needed:* yes → discount 0.5 already applied.

**H2 — 4.4 · authn** (`/api/v3/chatbase`)
Anonymous `POST` reaches the model and returns an answer, giving free LLM capacity on the vendor's account plus controllable prompt surface. *Evidence:* bundle-only today — my paired control shows GET/HEAD/OPTIONS **cannot** distinguish this route from a nonexistent path, so route existence is not yet proven by any read. *Refuted if:* `401`/`403` without a session, or 404 when unauthenticated.

**H3 — 4.0 · info-disclosure** (`main-layout-B9P_srS5.css`)
A style nonce inlined in a client-reachable stylesheet enables cross-style exfiltration of CSRF-protected HTML. *Refuted if:* no nonce in the CSS, or the nonce never matches the served HTML.

---

**[PROBE]** — read-only, `≤1 rps`, GET/HEAD/OPTIONS only. No POST was issued this cycle.

- `api.hypofriend.de` → `52.15.184.3`; HTTPS+HTTP HEAD `000` at 8s. 50+ cycles dead. Not re-probed further.
- Paired routing control on the same endpoint family (1 req per path, 2s apart):
  `/api/v3/chatbase` → `301`, 0 B · `/api/v3/zzz-nonexistent-9f2` → `301`, 0 B · `/api/v3/advisors` → `401`, 27 B.
- `OPTIONS /api/v3/chatbase` (hostile `Origin: https://evil.example`) → `200`, reflects the origin, `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, all 7 methods, `content-type`, `max-age 7200`. This is **global rack-cors behavior**, identical to the previously-established precondition — not a new finding.
- Static render-path audit of the chat widget (local file, no traffic):
  `innerHTML:o.renderMarkdown(...)` at two sinks, and `renderMarkdown = x1 = w0.parse(...); return L1.sanitize(t,T1)` with `ALLOWED_TAGS:[p,br,strong,b,em,i,h1..h4,ul,ol,li,a,code,pre,blockquote,table,thead,tbody,tr,th,td,hr,del]`, `ALLOWED_ATTR:[href,target,rel,title]`. `userContent` is entity-escaped then `L1.sanitize`d. **No `v-html`, no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.** Chat XSS: **REJECTED** with a control, not on absence of evidence.
- One `grep -oE` sweep exceeded 120s; recovered with a Python byte-offset extractor. No scope breach.

---

**[PARKED]**

- `exposeSeen` / `sendVerifiedListingEmail` / document+offer mutations — the deciding step is a state-changing POST on live customer records. Out of scope this cycle. **HUMAN_ONLY**, needs an authorized test lead + control inbox.
- `unclaimLead` (session-scoped), `createUploadUrl` (301), `/api/v3/delete-cookie` (rejected logout-CSRF class), `min-width:300px` responsive-image breakpoint (scanner noise).
- Full-PII read on `property-search-api` — confirmed critical last cycle, already in the report; re-validating it now would mean touching customer data for no new information.

---

**[FINAL]** — what I would actually put in the report today

Nothing new clears the bar this cycle. The eight existing findings stand unchanged; `/api/v3/chatbase` is a **new, unreported** surface whose only remaining question is one POST, and its two most attractive sub-stories (chat XSS, link-preview SSRF) are **closed with controls** and must not appear in the report. The high-signal fact is the deploy cadence: 8 bundle renames in 48h, each of which invalidates a hash-pinned finding within hours.

---

**[NEXT]** — exactly one

```
GET https://hypofriend.de/en/account/assets/index-BLX7yr-v.js.map
Host: hypofriend.de
Accept: */*
```
Passive, single request. Vite frequently ships `.map` beside the hashed asset and the bundle carries no `sourceMappingURL` to tell us either way. A hit yields the unminified sources — the exact `/api/v3/chatbase` request contract, the `identity` payload, and any server-side constant. A `404` retires the sourcemap angle in one shot. Fetch as `Accept-Charset: ISO-8859-1` to avoid UTF-8 C1 misinterpretation, and compare `Content-Length` against 2,468,656 to confirm you are not being served the JS under a `.map` name.

---

**[LEARN] / [RISK]**

- An `OPTIONS` origin reflection is a **precondition, never a finding** — it only becomes one when chained to a route that mutates or returns sensitive data (`property-search-api`, `/my_app/api/v2/lead/session`, `/api/v3/advisors`). I nearly filed chatbase's CORS as a finding; the paired control is what stopped it.
- Never infer XSS from `innerHTML`. Read the sink's producer: both chat render paths terminate in a DOMPurify allowlist that omits `img`, `script`, and every event handler.
- "Referenced by today's bundle" ≠ "reachable". The `/auth/google_oauth2` mistake, nearly repeated.
- Agent IDs in a public bundle are public by construction. Hashing them satisfies the secret-handling rule without inventing a credential finding.
- `max-age 7200` means any CORS pair stays browser-cacheable for two hours — treat that as a replay window when you document the CORS chain.
- Residual risk: if `/api/v3/chatbase` is in fact unauthenticated, the impact is cost amplification plus attacker-shaped content rendered inside a real lead's authenticated session, with `leadId`/`givenName` handed to the model. That is why the next test needs an authorized test account and never a real lead.
