[HYP] The redirect gate on object-addressing mutations may enforce session presence without ownership, leaving cross-tenant object addressing open
class: IDOR
asset: core.hypofriend.de/q
confidence: 58
reasoning: Four mutations address objects by caller-supplied identifiers and none carries a lead or tenant argument. Every one of them returns `301 Location: https://hypofriend.de/` with an empty body once its input coerces; no 401 or 403 was observed on any mutation, so the only observed gate is a redirect to a marketing page. The read path with caller-supplied identifiers is open in the same deployment: `exposeSeen(zero,zero)` returned `200 {"seen":false}` unauthenticated. The `/en/account` bundle records `offerId` as client-visible response data, so at least one of these identifiers is obtainable by a browser.
evidence_needed: an authenticated call with an owned identifier that returns `200` while the same mutation with a foreign identifier returns an authorization error rather than a redirect; or any 401/403 anywhere on the mutation surface; or a non-redirect error body naming the record.
verify_steps: 1) re-issue `POST https://core.hypofriend.de/q` with body `{"query":"mutation { selectOffer(input:{offerProductId:\"zzz-nonexistent-9f2c\"}) { __typename } }"}` **after** establishing a session jar via a prior `GET /`, and compare status and `Location` against the no-cookie 301 already recorded; 2) with a real account, call the same mutation with an owned `offerProductId` and record whether the response is 200 or 301; 3) repeat with an identifier belonging to a different lead and compare.
impact: if authorization is absent, an authenticated customer could delete another lead's document attachments, mutate another lead's property record, and select offer products by identifier on a mortgage platform — document destruction plus money-adjacent state change, high severity. Nil if the redirect is a hard login gate.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

