## [HYP]

### H1 — `*.app.hypofriend.de` wholesale hijack via EIP release (REFRAMED)
- **Mechanism:** a zone-owned wildcard cannot be "claimed" the classic way, but a wildcard pinned to a single unclaimed EIP means that if `52.14.251.223` is ever released, **every** `*.app` name silently follows to whoever claims the IP — one event, not N. Paired with the co-dead `api.hypofriend.de` us-east-2 EIP, a coordinated teardown is plausible.
- **Vector:** watch both us-east-2 EIPs for release → re-register the IP → wildcard follows → serve `*.app` under our control (phishing the mobile/app auth surface).
- **Evidence:** wildcard confirmed on single NS; both us-east-2 hosts TCP-dead; all other 9+ fleet IPs live in eu-central-1.
- **Gate:** cloud-side EIP ownership visibility (not black-box); DNS-control is the *precondition*, exploit is silent wildcard follow.
- **Score:** attack_surface 6 (one record, no live service) · business 6 (whole app namespace, single event) · technical 7 · **gate 9 (wholesale follow, strongest yet)** · cloud 9 · freshness 9 (just discovered) → **7.4**.

### H2 — `graph.hypofriend.de` GraphQL at apex (NEW, ties to strongest prior work)
- **Mechanism:** a GraphQL endpoint at the apex brand domain. Prior `/q` work (error-channel schema reconstruction, disabled introspection, `root.form_authenticity_token`, `root.lead.has_admin_cookie`) makes this the single highest-leverage new target.
- **Vector:** disabled-introspection error-channel reconstruction → enumerate schema → object-level authz → resolve caller-supplied IDs to tenant data.
- **Evidence:** name resolves into the wildcard ELB pool (503); historical passive record placed it at 3.125.177.135 (shared dev box with `experiments`/`landingpage`/`local`).
- **Gate:** needs live 200; currently ELB 503.
- **Score:** attack_surface 8 (apex + GraphQL) · business 8 · technical 8 · gate 6 (needs live200) · cloud 7 · freshness 9 → **7.6**.

